  "question": {
    "text": "Two vertical poles&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>m</mi></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>m</mi></math>&nbsp; are standing apart on a horizontal ground with points <em><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math></em> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>C</mi></math> on the ground. If&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math> is the point of intersection of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo></math> then the height of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math> (in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi></math> ) above the line&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi></math> is :"
  },
